From: Paulo Duarte <paulofernandobd@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 31 May 2026 02:06:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] elf/rtld: walk _environ directly in process_envvars_secure (Hurd link fix).

The plain `getenv(nextp)` call inside process_envvars_secure (added in
glibc commit 59974938fe1f4add, 2024-04-16) is unresolved when linking
ld.so: ld.so does not link against libc, and the elf/Makefile
rtld-libc.a discovery pass does not always pull stdlib/getenv.os in via
the libc_pic.a probe.  A Linux host happens to satisfy this through its
libc_pic.a symbol-table walk; cross-building from a non-Linux host
(e.g. macOS) trips with `undefined reference to 'getenv'` at the ld.so
link, which is done with -Wl,-z,defs (no undefined symbols permitted).

Fix: walk _environ directly.  _environ, strlen, strncmp, and unsetenv
are all already used in this same function, so this adds no new
link-time dependency.  The semantic is identical: increment skip_env
iff the named variable is set, then unsetenv it.  Correct on every
port, so upstreamable as-is.
---
 elf/rtld.c |   20 ++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

--- a/elf/rtld.c
+++ b/elf/rtld.c
@@ -2555,12 +2555,20 @@
   do
     {
       /* Keep track of the number of environment variables that were set in
-         the environment and are unset below.  Use getenv() which returns
-	 non-NULL if the variable is set in the environment.  This count is
-	 needed if we need to adjust the location of the AUX vector on the
-	 stack when running ld.so directly. */
-      if (getenv (nextp) != NULL)
-        skip_env++;
+         the environment and are unset below.  Walk _environ directly
+         instead of calling getenv(): on the GNU Hurd, ld.so does not link
+         against libc, so the elf/Makefile rtld-libc.a discovery pass does
+         not always pull stdlib/getenv.os in (the Linux build happens to,
+         via a libc_pic.a quirk we don't share).  The count feeds into the
+         auxv shuffle in _dl_start_args_adjust when running ld.so directly,
+         so a working substitute is required, not a stub returning NULL.  */
+      size_t nlen = strlen (nextp);
+      for (char **ep = _environ; *ep != NULL; ++ep)
+	if (strncmp (*ep, nextp, nlen) == 0 && (*ep)[nlen] == '=')
+	  {
+	    skip_env++;
+	    break;
+	  }
 
       unsetenv (nextp);
       nextp = strchr (nextp, '\0') + 1;
